The window bundles SolidJS (MIT) and the IBM Plex fonts (SIL Open Font Licence 1.1). The desktop
shell uses Tauri (MIT or Apache-2.0) and the Rust crates listed in Cargo.lock, each under MIT,
Apache-2.0, BSD, ISC, Zlib, MPL-2.0 or Unicode licences (checked by cargo deny, deny.toml). Excel
workbooks are written with rust_xlsxwriter (MIT or Apache-2.0), as in Datum Plan, and datasheets are
read back from Excel with calamine (MIT); DEXPI files are read with quick-xml (MIT), which calamine
also uses. calamine brings encoding_rs ((Apache-2.0 or MIT) and
BSD-3-Clause), codepage, atoi_simd, fast-float2, simdutf8, core_detect, debug_unsafe and
multiversion_no_op (each MIT or Apache-2.0). The project file is
an SQLite database (SQLite is in the public domain, https://sqlite.org/copyright.html), read and written
with rusqlite (MIT), which builds SQLite into the app.

The symbols are Datum Schema's own vector drawings, following the conventions of the public
symbol standards ISO 10628-2 (equipment, valves, fittings and lines) and ISA-5.1 (instrument
bubbles and letter codes). No symbol library from other software is copied.

The unit table (crates/core/src/units.rs) is copied from Datum Calc (MIT, same author), by way of
Datum Pipe, with factors from NIST SP 811 (2008). Pipe outside diameters and wall thicknesses
(crates/core/src/pipes.rs, from Datum Pipe) are the published nominal dimensions of wrought steel
pipe (ASME B36.10M) and stainless steel pipe (ASME B36.19M). The line hydraulics use the
Darcy–Weisbach equation and the Colebrook equation (C. F. Colebrook, J. Inst. Civil Eng. 11
(1939) 133–156), as in Datum Calc's pipe pressure-drop pack.

The family window, ribbon, File page, updater and printing are taken from Datum Plan; the drawing
canvas and the orthogonal router from Datum Sim (all MIT, same author).
